# Yuriko

**Yuriko** runs an Xbox Series title natively on Windows, from a copy of the game you supply. There
is no CPU emulation: the title's own x86-64 code runs on the PC, its console system libraries are
reimplemented, and its RDNA2 GPU programs are translated to DXIL for Direct3D 12 when the title
creates them. Yuriko is a fork of [VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory) that brings
together the best work of every project on the same title (see [Credits](#credits)), tuned for
**NVIDIA** cards (32-lane waves, the 1,000,000-view descriptor heap cap), and measured run by run.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **This repository contains no game files, code or assets, and never will.** You need your own
> copy of the game. Yuriko does not download, decrypt or unpack game packages and contains nothing
> for circumventing DRM. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the game's developer or publisher.
> The title is not named anywhere in this repository on purpose.

## Status

<p align="center"><img src="assets/progress.svg" width="100%" alt="Progress map: every area of the port and how far along it is"></p>

**Not playable yet: about 7 frames a second in the first 3D scene, native renderer, no upscaling.**
Measured on one machine (Windows 11, NVIDIA RTX 5070 12 GB, Ryzen 5 7600X, 32 GB RAM), in
14-minute unattended runs. The live page, regenerated from the measurements at every publish, is
**[docs/STATUS.md](docs/STATUS.md)**: the latest run's frame breakdown, where the card's time goes,
every area with its evidence, and the history of every world run with the switches it set.

| | |
|---|---|
| Boot, first-run setup, menus | ✅ 30 fps |
| Intro video, loading screens | ✅ smooth, 30 fps |
| Shader translation | ✅ 25,735 of the shader pack's 25,985 containers translate offline (250 refused, down from 291); in the first scene every vertex, pixel and mesh program, all but 3 compute programs |
| The 3D world | ✅ drawn and lit: the opening cutscene, the first room, characters, the yard; gameplay starts and control is handed over |
| Frame rate (world) | 🚧 **~7 fps** (142.6-148.8 ms a frame); 5.5 fps before 2026-11-25's switch set; 7.8 fps with `--console series-s` (720p + upscale, opt-in) |
| Indirect light | 🚧 a constant stand-in; the title's ray-traced light needs the structure builder |
| Ray-traced light | 🚧 the structure builder runs without corrupting memory (`VICTORY_BVH_BUILDER=1`), but needs more than 32 GB in the current memory model: off |
| Stability | ✅ 14-minute world runs without a crash or device removal |
| Audio | ✅ menus, video, cutscenes |
| Beyond the prologue | ❌ not reached yet |

**The goal:** the whole game, playable, at **30 frames a second from the native renderer**.
Upscaling and frame generation are welcome as extras on top, never as the way to get there.

## Highlights

<table>
  <tr>
    <td width="33%" valign="top"><b>No CPU emulation.</b> The title's x86-64 code runs as it is; each call into a console library lands in Yuriko's own implementation of it (loader, kernel, memory, files, DirectStorage with zlib and BCPack, audio, input).</td>
    <td width="33%" valign="top"><b>RDNA2 → DXIL, directly.</b> GPU programs are decoded from the console's machine code and written as DXIL bitcode, with no HLSL step; console 64-lane waves run as two 32-lane waves on NVIDIA where a program needs its whole wave (<a href="docs/wave64-on-wave32.md">how</a>).</td>
    <td width="33%" valign="top"><b>Pre-built pipelines.</b> <code>victory translate --cache --pipelines</code> translates the shader pack and makes ~27,100 host pipelines ahead of time, so the world never stalls on a compile.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td valign="top"><b>Shared memory without shared memory.</b> On the console the CPU and GPU share memory; here every write one side makes and the other reads is noticed and moved (write watch, guards, copies on the card, hashes, copy-back on its own thread). Most of the frame-rate work is making that cheaper.</td>
    <td valign="top"><b>Measured, not guessed.</b> Every change is a 14-minute unattended world run with a report: frame time and its parts, captured frames, every shader by name, memory over time, and with <code>-Diag</code> a sampling profiler, GPU time per pipeline and frame shots (<a href="#diagnostics">Diagnostics</a>).</td>
    <td valign="top"><b>The best of every project.</b> VIctory, fxceless/victory-fork, VIcky, ka1qux, DexNom, Belobug, flamingo, r4tedx and others, merged and credited; a scan of every project on the same title (and the <a href="https://portwatch-vi.xyz">PortWatch</a> board) runs before any new work.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

## How it works

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph game["your copy of the game (outside the repository)"]
    exe["title executable<br/>(x86-64)"]
    pack["shader pack,<br/>archives, video"]
  end
  exe --> loader["victory-loader<br/>map, relocate, bind imports"]
  loader --> code["title code runs natively"]
  code -- "console APIs" --> hle["victory-hle<br/>kernel, memory, files,<br/>DirectStorage, audio, input"]
  code -- "D3D12.X command lists" --> gpu["victory-gpu<br/>D3D12.X → D3D12, title memory,<br/>descriptors, copies, copy-back"]
  gpu -- "pipelines" --> shader["victory-shader<br/>RDNA2 → DXIL"]
  pack --> shader
  shader <--> cache[("shader + pipeline<br/>cache")]
  gpu --> d3d["Direct3D 12<br/>on the PC's card"]
```

The title's code runs on the CPU as it is. Each call into a console library lands in Yuriko's
implementation of it. Each GPU program is translated once, when the title creates a pipeline from
its bytes, and cached under a key of the translator's own identity, so a rebuild that does not
touch the translator keeps the cache. The details: [docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md),
the memory model and its plan: [docs/yuriko-memory-plan.md](docs/yuriko-memory-plan.md), ray
tracing: [docs/fork-ray-tracing.md](docs/fork-ray-tracing.md), where a world frame's time goes:
[docs/frame-time.md](docs/frame-time.md).

## Quick start

1. **Build** (Windows 10/11 x64, Rust; the pinned toolchain installs itself through `rustup`):

   ```powershell
   cargo build --release -p victory                                # target\release\victory.exe (play)
   cargo build --profile diag -p victory --features diagnostics    # target\diag\victory.exe (logs, captures, instruments)
   ```

2. **Prepare** (once per translator change, 20-30 minutes, nothing else running): translate the
   title's shaders and pre-build its pipelines.

   ```powershell
   $env:VICTORY_CACHE_DIR = "D:\yuriko-data\cache"   # any folder outside the repository
   $env:VICTORY_DATA_DIR  = "D:\yuriko-data\data"
   target\release\victory.exe translate --cache --pipelines --jobs 6 "<your game folder>"
   ```

3. **Run:**

   ```powershell
   target\release\victory.exe run "<your game folder>\<the title's executable>" --execute
   ```

Yuriko sets its measured speed switches itself (`YURIKO_DEFAULTS=0` turns them all off). The
first boot shows the title's setup pages; a gamepad is recommended. The game folder is never
modified.

## What works

✅ done and verified · 🚧 partly · ❌ not yet. Evidence for every line: [docs/STATUS.md](docs/STATUS.md#areas).

<details>
<summary><b>Running the title's code</b>: loader, kernel, memory, files, DirectStorage</summary>

| Part | State | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| PE loader | ✅ | maps the executable and its DLLs, relocations, imports by name and ordinal, TLS, exception tables; 0 missing imports |
| Kernel | ✅ | threads on the PC's cores, synchronisation, TLS, time, events; no hangs in 14-minute runs |
| Title memory | ✅ | the console's virtual and physical memory APIs, private arenas, GPU-visible ranges |
| Files | ✅ | console paths to the game folder; writes go to the data folder, never the game's |
| DirectStorage | ✅ | file and memory sources, zlib, BCPack (BC1, BC3-BC7); 4 GB of DirectStorage writes per run land where the title mapped them |
| Saves, settings | ✅ | persistent local storage in `VICTORY_DATA_DIR` |
| Input | 🚧 | keyboard and gamepad; controls not yet checked through a whole session |
| Online services | ➖ | offline by design |

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Graphics</b>: command lists, descriptors, resources, presentation</summary>

| Part | State | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| D3D12.X command lists | ✅ | draws, mesh draws, dispatches, indirect (with windows of clamped records), copies, barriers, queries |
| Descriptors | ✅ | the title's descriptor records, including those it copies as bytes, mirrored into host heaps; NVIDIA's 1,000,000-view cap handled by a heap map (VIcky) |
| Textures | ✅ | GFX10 tiling and mips detiled; refreshed when the title writes them; levels on demand |
| Title buffers | ✅ | copies on the card, refreshed by what the title wrote (write watch, guards) and compared after shaders write; copy-back on its own thread and copy queue |
| Presentation | ✅ | 30 fps pacing in menus; Series S second plane and upscale stretch (`--console series-s`) |
| Ray tracing | 🚧 | acceleration-structure builder runs and draws (ka1qux's fixes), memory-bound in the copy model: off by default |

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Shaders</b>: RDNA2 → DXIL</summary>

| Part | State | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Vertex, pixel, mesh | ✅ | every program the first scene uses translates |
| Compute | 🚧 | all but 3 in the first scene (a descriptor's address read as data; two wave64 groups over 64 threads with shared memory) |
| Wave64 on 32-lane hosts | ✅ | whole-wave programs run as two host waves, cross-half exchanges where needed ([docs/wave64-on-wave32.md](docs/wave64-on-wave32.md)) |
| DXIL clean-up | ✅ | dead code, constants, value reuse, branch facts, load forwarding, loop-invariant motion, barrier elimination (`VICTORY_SHADER_OPT`) |
| Cache | ✅ | translations keyed by the translator's source identity and every switch that changes a translation |

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>Audio, video</b></summary>

| Part | State | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| Audio | ✅ | the title's mixer output; menus, video and cutscenes play |
| XMA | 🚧 | decoded; some keep-up issues under load |
| Video | ✅ | the intro and in-engine movies play smoothly |

</details>

## Where a frame goes

A world frame on the RTX 5070 (latest numbers in [docs/STATUS.md](docs/STATUS.md); the full account
and the levers in progress in [docs/frame-time.md](docs/frame-time.md)):

<p align="center"><img src="assets/perf-history.svg" width="100%" alt="World frame time of every plain run, with the 30 fps native target"></p>

| part | ms | what it is | the lever |
|---|---|---|---|
| preparing (CPU) | ~52 | turning the title's lists into host lists: copies of title memory (~37), texture refreshes (~12), descriptors | fewer bytes moved (landings not echoed, draws' tables left to the draws), more threads |
| waiting for the card | ~76 | the submitting thread waiting for submissions to finish (mostly 2 settles a frame for title buffer copies that read memory the card may still write, ~78 ms; the card is busy meanwhile, so this is mostly the GPU being the wall) | copies done when their submission lands (`VICTORY_LATE_COPY`), fills that wait only for the bytes they read |
| the card's own work | busy ~85 % of the frame | the title's lists (~82 ms of card time) (single passes up to 8.7 ms: soft shadow masks, light-probe allocation) plus our hash and compare passes | cheaper translated programs, fewer passes of ours, read-back on demand |
| copy-back | ~35 | bringing back what shaders wrote, overlapped on its own thread | in-place title memory (memory plan stages 3-4) |

The frame time is about the larger of preparing and GPU plus what cannot overlap; 30 fps needs
both under ~33 ms. Every project on the same title measures the same four costs: **noticing** the
title's writes, **noticing** the shaders' writes, **moving** them, and **waiting** for order.

## Diagnostics

Every run of the diagnostics build writes a frame log; `tools/run_victory.ps1` runs the title
unattended and reports. `-Diag` turns every instrument on:

```powershell
tools\run_victory.ps1 -Exe target\diag\victory.exe -Seconds 840 -Tag mytest          # a plain, timed run
tools\run_victory.ps1 -Exe target\diag\victory.exe -Seconds 840 -Tag mytest -Diag    # every instrument (slower)
```

| file in the run folder | what it holds |
|---|---|
| `summary.txt` | read first: world reached, ms/frame and its parts, draws, drops, refused programs |
| `report.png`, `report.html` | one page of charts and frames; every shader by name, render-target gallery, op lists, warnings |
| `diagnostics.md` | the frame log's newest word on everything (settles, relay, copies, textures, descriptors, write watch), the slowest windows, and with `-Diag`: **GPU time by pipeline and by list**, the **profiler**, **frame shots**, checks, device-removed data, driver events |
| `profile.txt`, `profile.folded` | `-Diag`: where every thread spends its time; the `.folded` file feeds a flame graph |
| `shot/shot-*` | `-Diag`: one frame taken apart: every command with its GPU timestamp, and the picture each pass left |
| `frames/`, `mem.csv`, `problems.json` | captured frames, memory over time, everything flagged |

Every switch the runtime reads, with what it does and whether it is on by default:
**[docs/switches.md](docs/switches.md)** (generated from the source). How to read all of it:
**[docs/diagnostics.md](docs/diagnostics.md)**.

## Roadmap

1. **Frame rate, native** (now ~7 fps → 30): move fewer bytes (landings not echoed, draws' tables
   left to the draws, `VICTORY_THIN`-style recording on many threads), cut the top GPU passes, then
   in-place title memory (memory plan stage 3).
2. **Ray-traced light**: the builder works; it needs the in-place memory model to fit in 32 GB.
3. **The 3 refused compute programs** (multi-wave groups with shared memory on 32-lane hosts).
4. **Playability**: controls through a whole session, stability over an hour, missions past the
   prologue.
5. **Extras on top of native 30 fps**: DLSS / FSR upscaling and frame generation (branch work, off
   by default).

## Help wanted

- **Test on your card** and file a [test report](../../issues/new?template=test_report.md): AMD
  cards (64-lane waves), other NVIDIA generations, more RAM. Attach `summary.txt` and
  `diagnostics.md` from a `-Diag` run; never attach anything from the game.
- **Take a GPU pass apart**: `diagnostics.md` names the pipelines that take the card longest.
- **Port what works elsewhere**: the [PortWatch board](https://portwatch-vi.xyz) lists every effort
  on the same title; `tools/upstream_scan.py` shows what changed since the last look.

Read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) first. One change per pull request, measured with a world
run before and after, and no game data of any kind in the repository.

## Asset policy

Nothing from the game is ever stored here: no assets, no executable code or data, nothing derived
from them (shader dumps, captures, extracted tables). See [docs/asset-policy.md](docs/asset-policy.md);
`cargo xtask policy` checks it and runs as a pre-commit hook (`cargo xtask install-hooks`) and in CI.

## Credits

Yuriko is built on the work of many people; [CREDITS.md](CREDITS.md) names them and what came from
each. In short: [VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory) (Marvisak and contributors) is the
base; [fxceless/victory-fork](https://github.com/fxceless/victory-fork) drew the world first, and
most of the world rendering and frame-rate work comes from it; the NVIDIA heap map from
[VIcky](https://github.com/Xeltax/VIcky); wave64 on 32-lane hosts from r4tedx and others;
ray-tracing fixes and translator work from [ka1qux](https://github.com/ka1qux/BRVIctory-ka1qux) and
DexNom; the indirect-light stand-in from [Belobug](https://github.com/Belobug/VIctory-Belobug-AMD);
Series S presentation from victory-fork PR #188 and [flamingo](https://github.com/realblastex/flamingo);
measurements and findings from [avatarmanz](https://github.com/avatarmanz/VIctory-7900XTX).
[XCL](https://github.com/xcl-dev/xcl) is a separate project with the same aim; its notes and one
ported file are credited in CREDITS.md.

